{"type":"message","message":{"id":"gen-1775332994-x0tNTI8l9ZSwbYs4666e","role":"assistant","created":1775333001,"content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I'm getting closer to understanding the picture. Let me analyze what I've found:\n\nFrom the 1962 Treasury Bulletin (page 82), I see in the footnotes:\n- \"The Secretary of the Treasury determined on October 20, 1961, that $1 million of Treasury notes of 1890 have been destroyed or irretrievably lost and so will never be presented for redemption.\"\n\nFrom the 1963 Treasury Bulletin (page 88), I see:\n- \"On October 20, 1961, $1 million of Treasury notes of 1890; and on August 27, 1962, $9 million of gold certificates, $15 million of silver certificates, $18 million of Federal Reserve notes, $1 million of Federal Reserve Bank notes, and $15 million of national bank notes.\"\n\nSo the Treasury notes of 1890 were being retired over time. The key date seems to be October 20, 1961, when $1 million was determined to be destroyed. But wait, that's just the amount that was determined to be destroyed in 1961 - it doesn't mean that's when they were removed from the ledgers.\n\nLooking at the tables:\n- In 1961: Treasury notes of 1890 = $2,094 million (June 1961)\n- In 1962: Treasury notes of 1890 = $2,009 million (June 1962)\n\nWait, looking at the monthly data:\n- 1961-November: $2,098 million (Treasury notes of 1890)\n- 1961-October: $2,076 million \n- 1962-August: $1,945 million\n- 1962-September: $1,943 million\n\nWait, let me re-read the question: \"In the calendar year that the treasury notes of 1890 were removed from the U.S federal government ledgers...\"\n\nI need to understand when they were \"removed\" from the ledgers. \n\nI see a pattern of gradual reduction in the Treasury notes of 1890. The footnote reveals a formal process of retirement and destruction, starting from March 1900. The notes were systematically taken out of circulation, with specific amounts being officially recognized as destroyed or lost at different points in time. This suggests a long-term, methodical process of removing these historical currency notes from the financial system.\n","signature":""},{"type":"toolRequest","id":"call_55055d4aa64940f2b46fa9c9","toolCall":{"status":"success","value":{"name":"shell","arguments":{"command":"grep -r \"1890\" /app/resources/ --include=\"*.txt\" --include=\"*.json\" | head -50"}}},"_meta":{"goose_extension":"developer"}}],"metadata":{"userVisible":true,"agentVisible":true}}}
