{"type":"message","message":{"id":"gen-1775333005-AeNlzSugXMGZyb5rRp08","role":"assistant","created":1775333014,"content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me analyze what I found. The Treasury notes of 1890 have been in process of retirement since March 1900. Looking at the data, the Treasury notes of 1890 amounts from the tables are:\n\n1961: 2,094 million\n1962: 2,009 million\n\nAnd in the monthly data for 1962:\n- January: 1,974\n- February: 1,969\n- March: 2,013\n- April: 2,030\n- May: 2,035\n- June: 2,009\n- July: 1,980\n- August: 1,945\n- September: 1,943\n- October: 1,947\n- November: 1,957\n\nWait, there's an interesting pattern here. The data shows that in August 1962, there's a significant drop in \"Gold certificates\" from 29 to 20 million, and also a significant drop in \"National bank notes\" from 53 to 38 million. But these seem to be related to the destruction of these notes as mentioned in the footnotes.\n\nLooking at the footnotes in the 1963 bulletin:\n- On October 20, 1961, $1 million of Treasury notes of 1890 were determined to be destroyed\n- On August 27, 1962, $9 million of gold certificates, $15 million of silver certificates, $18 million of Federal Reserve notes, $1 million of Federal Reserve Bank notes, and $15 million of national bank notes were determined to be destroyed\n\nBut this seems to be related to the \"destruction\" of notes that were already removed from circulation. The question is asking when the Treasury notes of 1890 were \"removed from the U.S. federal government ledgers\" and how much paper money was added in circulation during that calendar year.\n\nHmm, let me look at this differently. I need to find when the Treasury notes of 1890 reached $0 (or were fully retired). Looking at the data:\n- 1961: 2,094 (fiscal year 1961)\n\n\n- 1962: 2,009 (fiscal year 1962)\n\nI'm tracking the gradual reduction of these treasury notes. In 1962, the notes steadily decreased from 1,974 million in January to 1,943 million in September. This suggests a consistent process of retirement and removal from circulation. The small incremental drops indicate a methodical approach to eliminating these older treasury notes over time.\n\nHowever, I'm puzzled because the figures haven't reached zero. The footnotes mention $1 million destroyed in October 1961, which doesn't fully explain the remaining balance. I need to investigate whether there might be additional data beyond November 1962 that could clarify the complete retirement of these notes.\n","signature":""},{"type":"toolRequest","id":"call_6876c3bb7e4043928c04771a","toolCall":{"status":"success","value":{"name":"shell","arguments":{"command":"ls -la /app/resources/"}}},"_meta":{"goose_extension":"developer"}}],"metadata":{"userVisible":true,"agentVisible":true}}}
