{"type":"message","message":{"id":"gen-1775333014-4BuglmKzJRaKvXikutB4","role":"assistant","created":1775333021,"content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Looking at the data, I can see:\n1. Treasury notes of 1890 appear as a category in the paper money tables\n2. From the footnotes, we see they have been in process of retirement since March 1900\n\nLooking at the data:\n- In 1961 (fiscal year): Treasury notes of 1890 = $2,094 million\n- In 1962 (fiscal year): Treasury notes of 1890 = $2,009 million\n\nThe question asks when they were \"removed from the U.S. federal government ledgers\" - this suggests when they were fully retired/removed.\n\nLooking more carefully at the data, I need to find when the Treasury notes of 1890 went to zero or were removed. Let me look at more recent data to see when they finally reached zero.\n\nActually, looking at the tables:\n- From 1962: The last time Treasury notes of 1890 appear as a specific entry is in the data\n- The question mentions \"removed from U.S. federal government ledgers\" - this could be referring to when they were officially removed as a category\n\nLet me look at the 1963 data more carefully. I see that in November 1962, Treasury notes of 1890 shows 1,957 (in millions).\n\nWait, the question is asking about the calendar year when they were removed, and how much paper money was added in circulation during that year.\n\nActually, wait. Looking at the data again:\n- The 1962 bulletin shows data up to November 1962\n- The 1963 bulletin shows data up to November 1963\n\nI should look more carefully. The Treasury notes of 1890 were being retired gradually. They didn't just disappear in one year.\n\nBut wait - looking at the footnotes again:\n- \"The Secretary of the Treasury determined on October 20, 1961, that $1 million of Treasury notes of 1890 have been destroyed or irretrievably lost\"\n\nThis seems to suggest they were marked as retired in 1961.\n\nHowever, I'm not seeing when they reached zero. Let me search for more specific information or look at more recent data.\n\n\nI'm investigating the specific year of removal from government ledgers. The footnote reveals a $1 million destruction in 1961, but doesn't indicate complete removal. The data shows a gradual decline from $2,094 million in 1961 to $1,957 million in November 1962, suggesting a progressive retirement process. I'll need to examine later data to determine the exact year of final removal.\n","signature":""},{"type":"toolRequest","id":"call_d72269ff2afa48d98a0aba5b","toolCall":{"status":"success","value":{"name":"shell","arguments":{"command":"grep -n \"Treasury notes of 1890\" /app/resources/treasury_bulletin_1963_01.txt | head -30"}}},"_meta":{"goose_extension":"developer"}}],"metadata":{"userVisible":true,"agentVisible":true}}}
{"type":"message","message":{"id":"msg_0dd05bc2-0e0d-4feb-bc47-e0228947f728","role":"user","created":1775333021,"content":[{"type":"toolResponse","id":"call_d72269ff2afa48d98a0aba5b","toolResult":{"status":"success","value":{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"4343:| End of fiscal year or month > Unnamed: 0_level_1 | Total money in circulation 1/ > Unnamed: 1_level_1 | Paper money 2/ > Total paper money | Paper money 2/ > Gold certificates | Paper money 2/ > Silver certificates | Paper money 2/ > Treasury notes of 1890 | Paper money 2/ > United States notes | Paper money 2/ > Federal Reserve notes | Paper money 2/ > Federal Reserve Bank notes | Unnamed: 9_level_0 > National bank notes |\n4391:2/ The following paper currencies are in process of retirement and are redeemable from the general fund of the Treasury: Gold certificates issued before January 30, 1934, silver certificates issued before July 1, 1929, Treasury notes of 1890, Federal Reserve notes issued before the series of 1928, Federal Reserve Bank notes, and national bank notes. The Treasury notes of 1890 have been in process of retirement since March 1900 (31 U.S.C. 411) upon receipt by the Treasury, the Federal Reserve Bank notes since June 12, 1945 (12 U.S.C. 445 note), and the national bank notes since December 23, 1915 (12 U.S.C. 441). Retirement of the others was authorized by the Old Series Currency Adjustment Act, approved June 30, 1961 (31 U.S.C. 912-916). The act authorizes the Secretary of the Treasury to determine from time to time the amount of currency of the following types which have been destroyed or lost, and to reduce the amounts thereof on the books of the Treasury: Gold certificates issued before January 30, 1934; silver certificates, United States notes, Federal Reserve Bank\n4393:notes, and national bank notes all issued before July 1, 1929; Federal Reserve notes issued prior to the series of 1928; and Treasury notes of 1890. Accordingly, the Secretary of the Treasury has determined that the following paper currencies have been destroyed or irretreivably lost and so will never be presented for redemption: On October 20, 1961, $1 million of Treasury notes of 1890; and on August 27, 1962, $9 million of gold certificates, $15 million of silver certificates, $18 million of Federal Reserve notes, $1 million of Federal Reserve Bank notes, and $15 million of national bank notes.\n4465:of $156.0 million against United States notes and through June 30, 1961, Treasury notes of 1890.","annotations":{"priority":0.0}}],"structuredContent":{"stdout":"4343:| End of fiscal year or month > Unnamed: 0_level_1 | Total money in circulation 1/ > Unnamed: 1_level_1 | Paper money 2/ > Total paper money | Paper money 2/ > Gold certificates | Paper money 2/ > Silver certificates | Paper money 2/ > Treasury notes of 1890 | Paper money 2/ > United States notes | Paper money 2/ > Federal Reserve notes | Paper money 2/ > Federal Reserve Bank notes | Unnamed: 9_level_0 > National bank notes |\n4391:2/ The following paper currencies are in process of retirement and are redeemable from the general fund of the Treasury: Gold certificates issued before January 30, 1934, silver certificates issued before July 1, 1929, Treasury notes of 1890, Federal Reserve notes issued before the series of 1928, Federal Reserve Bank notes, and national bank notes. The Treasury notes of 1890 have been in process of retirement since March 1900 (31 U.S.C. 411) upon receipt by the Treasury, the Federal Reserve Bank notes since June 12, 1945 (12 U.S.C. 445 note), and the national bank notes since December 23, 1915 (12 U.S.C. 441). Retirement of the others was authorized by the Old Series Currency Adjustment Act, approved June 30, 1961 (31 U.S.C. 912-916). The act authorizes the Secretary of the Treasury to determine from time to time the amount of currency of the following types which have been destroyed or lost, and to reduce the amounts thereof on the books of the Treasury: Gold certificates issued before January 30, 1934; silver certificates, United States notes, Federal Reserve Bank\n4393:notes, and national bank notes all issued before July 1, 1929; Federal Reserve notes issued prior to the series of 1928; and Treasury notes of 1890. Accordingly, the Secretary of the Treasury has determined that the following paper currencies have been destroyed or irretreivably lost and so will never be presented for redemption: On October 20, 1961, $1 million of Treasury notes of 1890; and on August 27, 1962, $9 million of gold certificates, $15 million of silver certificates, $18 million of Federal Reserve notes, $1 million of Federal Reserve Bank notes, and $15 million of national bank notes.\n4465:of $156.0 million against United States notes and through June 30, 1961, Treasury notes of 1890.","stderr":"","exit_code":0},"isError":false}}}],"metadata":{"userVisible":true,"agentVisible":true}}}
