 than $500,000\n- I'll need to confirm the exact calendar year and paper money circulation change carefully\n\nI'll examine the total paper money circulation data to understand the impact of this removal, tracking changes from June 1961 through November 1961 to identify the precise increase during this transition period.\n\nThe Treasury notes of 1890 dropped significantly in October 1961, reaching less than $500,000, which suggests a key monetary shift. By November 1961, they reappeared at $2,098 million, indicating some complexity in the removal process.\n\nThe footnotes reveal the Secretary of the Treasury had determined $1 million of these notes were destroyed in October 1961, but this doesn't fully explain the dramatic fluctuation in the table's values.\n\nI notice the asterisk appears in October 1961, marking a potential turning point in the currency's status, with subsequent months showing varying amounts. The 1962 fiscal year data confirms this pattern, with Treasury notes of 1890 consistently marked with an asterisk, suggesting ongoing changes in its presentation.\n\nThe footnote reveals a key detail: in October 1961, the Treasury determined $1 million of these notes were destroyed and would never be redeemed. This indicates the formal recognition of their removal from active circulation, though physical notes might still exist.\n\nI'm carefully reviewing the data to pinpoint the precise moment of removal from government ledgers, focusing on the nuanced details of fiscal documentation.\n\nExamining the monthly progression shows a dramatic decline: from $2,098 million in November 1961 to a trace amount in subsequent months, signaling the end of the Treasury notes' operational status.\n\nI'll analyze the paper money circulation data to determine the net change during this transition period. By comparing the January and February 1962 values, I can calculate the precise increase in paper currency.\n\nThe data reveals a slight fluctuation: January 1962 shows $30,222 million, while February records $30,319 million, indicating a modest $97 million rise in paper money circulation during this specific month.\n\nHowever, I need to examine the broader context of Treasury notes of 1890 removal. The most significant drop occurred between November 1961 and January 1962, when the amount transitioned from $2,098 to $1,974 million - a reduction of $124 million.\n\nInterestingly, this period coincided with paper money additions, as total paper money actually increased from $30,975 million in November 1961 to $30,222 million in January 1962, suggesting a net gain of $1,247 million.\n\nThe question specifically asks about paper money added during the calendar year of Treasury note removal. I'm exploring two potential interpretations: either the complete physical removal in 1961 or the final book reduction in 1962, which showed $1 million as destroyed.\n\nThe footnote reveals a nuanced timeline: on October 20, 1961, the Secretary determined $1 million of Treasury notes were destroyed, with this information documented in the 1962 bulletin. However, the asterisk marking in October 1961 suggests the removal might have occurred earlier.\n\nI need to carefully analyze when the Treasury notes were truly considered \"removed from the U.S. federal government ledgers\" and correlate that with the paper money circulation data.\n\nThe table shows a significant drop from $2,098 in November 1961 to an asterisk in October 1961, indicating a potential transition point. However, the subsequent reappearance of $1,974 in January 1962 complicates the interpretation of when the removal actually happened.\n\nThis requires a detailed examination of the fiscal year data to pinpoint the precise moment of currency retirement.\n","signature":""},{"type":"toolRequest","id":"call_c66bc494073e416a83b0f02e","toolCall":{"status":"success","value":{"name":"shell","arguments":{"command":"python3 -c \"\nimport re\n\n# Read the 1962 page 82 file\nwith open('/app/resources/t