{"type":"message","message":{"id":"gen-1775333139-hZiIFpxPBA1xyzIFWpNt","role":"assistant","created":1775333151,"content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I can see the paper money values for 1961:\n- December 1960: 30,442 million\n- November 1961: 30,975 million\n\nThe change in paper money from December 1960 to November 1961 represents how much was added in calendar year 1961.\n\n30,975 - 30,442 = 533 million\n\nBut wait, I should also check December 1961. Let me check the 1963 document which has December 1961 data:\n\nLooking at the 1963 page 88 data:\n- 1961-December: Paper money = 31,336\n\nSo the increase in calendar year 1961 (from December 1960 to December 1961):\n31,336 - 30,442 = 894 million\n\nBut wait, the treasury notes of 1890 were \"removed from the ledgers\" on October 20, 1961. So we're looking at calendar year 1961.\n\nLet me think about what the question is asking more carefully:\n- \"the treasury notes of 1890 were removed from the U.S federal government ledgers\" - this happened on October 20, 1961 when the Secretary determined $1 million were destroyed/lost. But also, the Treasury notes went from $2,120 million in December 1961 to \"*\" (less than $500,000) in fiscal year 1962.\n\nActually wait, I need to find when they were FULLY removed. Looking at the data:\n- In the 1962 bulletin, Treasury notes of 1890 are listed as $1 million (or *) for most of 1961-1962\n- But the footnote says on October 20, 1961, $1 million were determined to be destroyed\n\nActually, let me reconsider what \"removed from the ledgers\" means. It seems like the Treasury notes of 1890 had been \"in process of retirement since March 1900\" but the question might be asking when they were fully retired or removed from the records.\n\nLooking at the data more closely, it seems like the Treasury notes of 1890 dropped significantly:\n\n\nI notice a significant drop from November 1961 to December 1961. The Treasury notes went from $2,098 million to $2,120 million, then dramatically decreased to \"*\" (less than $500,000) in the 1962 fiscal year data. This suggests a major removal event occurred around November-December 1961.\n\nI'll investigate the precise timing of this removal and determine the paper money added during that specific calendar year.\n","signature":""},{"type":"toolRequest","id":"call_a722440ccfca4bc1be638ed1","toolCall":{"status":"success","value":{"name":"shell","arguments":{"command":"python3 -c \"\n# Looking at paper money added in 1961\n# December 1960: 30,442 million\n# December 1961: 31,336 million (from 1963 bulletin)\n# Increase = 31,336 - 30,442 = 894 million\n\n# But let's verify - we need to determine what year the treasury notes were removed\n# The footnote says October 20, 1961: \\$1 million of Treasury notes of 1890 destroyed\n# And by fiscal year 1962 (which ends June 30, 1962), they're essentially gone (*)\n\n# Let me check the data around November/December 1961 and into 1962\n# to find when they went to essentially zero\n\nwith open('/app/resources/treasury_bulletin_1963_01_page_88.txt', 'r') as f:\n    content = f.read()\n\nimport re\n# Get all monthly data\nrows = re.findall(r'<tr><td>([^<]+)</td><td>([^<]*)</td><td>([^<]*)</td><td>([^<]*)</td><td>([^<]*)</td><td>([^<]*)</td><td>([^<]*)</td><td>([^<]*)</td><td>([^<]*)</td><td>([^<]*)</td></tr>', content)\n\n# Print just the 1961-1962 treasury notes of 1890 values\nprint('Date           | T1890')\nprint('-' * 30)\nfor row in rows:\n    date = row[0].strip()\n    t1890 = row[4].strip()\n    # Show data around the transition\n    if '1961' in date or '1962' in date:\n        if '1963' not in date and '1960' not in date and '195' not in date:\n            print(f'{date:15} | {t1890}')\n\""}}},"_meta":{"goose_extension":"developer"}}],"metadata":{"userVisible":true,"agentVisible":true}}}
