{"type":"message","message":{"id":"gen-1775333181-D5UrLqn7R2kf1cOlfFuE","role":"assistant","created":1775333194,"content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me re-check the column order in the table. Looking at the header from page 88:\n\n`<tr><th>End of fiscal year or month</th><th>Total money in circulation 1/</th><th colspan=\"7\">Paper money 2/</th></tr><tr><th></th><th></th><th>Total paper money</th><th>Gold certificates</th><th>Silver certificates</th><th>Treasury notes of 1890</th><th>United States notes</th><th>Federal Reserve notes</th><th>Federal Reserve Bank notes</th><th>National bank notes</th></tr>`\n\nSo the columns are:\n1. Date (End of fiscal year or month)\n2. Total money in circulation \n3. Total paper money\n4. Gold certificates\n5. Silver certificates\n6. Treasury notes of 1890\n7. United States notes\n8. Federal Reserve notes\n9. Federal Reserve Bank notes\n10. National bank notes\n\nSo looking at the 1962 fiscal year row:\n`1962 | 33,770 | 31,117 | 29 | 2,009 | * | 318 | 28,622 | 85 | 53`\n\nThis means:\n- Total: 33,770\n- Total paper: 31,117\n- Gold: 29\n- Silver: 2,009\n- Treasury 1890: * (less than $500,000)\n- US Notes: 318\n- FR Notes: 28,622\n- FRB Notes: 85\n- NB Notes: 53\n\nWait, that doesn't make sense. Let me look at the pattern more carefully. Looking at the columns:\n- Gold should be decreasing from 34 to 29 (which makes sense)\n- Silver certificates should be around 2,000s (which matches 2,009)\n- Treasury 1890 should be around 1-2 (which should be much smaller)\n\n\nI see the Treasury notes of 1890 are now marked with an asterisk, indicating they're less than $500,000. This suggests these notes have effectively been removed from circulation. I'll need to investigate when exactly this occurred and confirm the paper money increase for that calendar year. The removal seems to have happened in fiscal 1962, which ends in June 1962, but I'll need to pinpoint the precise calendar year of removal.\n\nLooking at the Treasury Bulletin, I confirm that in October 1961, $1 million of Treasury notes of 1890 were determined to be destroyed or lost, effectively removing them from the ledgers. For the calendar year 1961, I calculate the paper money increase by comparing December 1960 and December 1961, which shows an increase of $894 million.\n","signature":""},{"type":"toolRequest","id":"call_3b9d5d8bd1d54f50896f0937","toolCall":{"status":"success","value":{"name":"shell","arguments":{"command":"python3 -c \"\n# Let me double-check the column mapping\n\nwith open('/app/resources/treasury_bulletin_1963_01_page_88.txt', 'r') as f:\n    content = f.read()\n\n# Find the header row\nimport re\nheader = re.search(r'<th[^>]*>.*?Treasury notes of 1890</th>', content)\nprint('Found header')\n\n# Let me extract a sample row and manually map\nrows = re.findall(r'<tr><td>([^<]+)</td><td>([^<]+)</td><td>([^<]+)</td><td>([^<]+)</td><td>([^<]+)</td><td>([^<]+)</td><td>([^<]+)</td><td>([^<]+)</td><td>([^<]+)</td><td>([^<]+)</td></tr>', content)\n\n# Let's look at the 1962 annual row \nfor row in rows:\n    if row[0].strip() == '1962':\n        print('1962 row:')\n        print(f'Col 0 (Date): {row[0]}')\n        print(f'Col 1 (Total): {row[1]}')\n        print(f'Col 2 (Paper Total): {row[2]}')\n        print(f'Col 3 (Gold): {row[3]}')\n        print(f'Col 4 (Silver): {row[4]}')\n        print(f'Col 5 (T1890): {row[5]}')\n        print(f'Col 6 (USNotes): {row[6]}')\n        print(f'Col 7 (FRNotes): {row[7]}')\n        print(f'Col 8 (FRBNotes): {row[8]}')\n        print(f'Col 9 (NBNotes): {row[9]}')\n        break\n\""}}},"_meta":{"goose_extension":"developer"}}],"metadata":{"userVisible":true,"agentVisible":true}}}
