This $1 million matches what appeared in December 1960, but I'm not seeing the full resolution of this discrepancy in the data.\n\nThe Treasury notes of 1890 had technically been in retirement since March 1900, yet they remained on the books for decades. The Old Series Currency Adjustment Act of June 30, 1961, appears to be the mechanism that finally resolved this—allowing the Secretary to write off currency that was destroyed or lost. The footnote from October 1961 indicates $1 million was determined to be destroyed at that point, and the records show the notes went to * (less than $500,000) by December 1961.\n\nSo the answer is 1961, with $894 million in paper money added to circulation that year.\n","signature":""},{"type":"toolRequest","id":"call_d36891fd69214259bdbfa30d","toolCall":{"status":"success","value":{"name":"write","arguments":{"path":"/app/answer.txt","content":"894"}}},"_meta":{"goose_extension":"developer"}}],"metadata":{"userVisible":true,"agentVisible":true}}}
